igma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.13, "baseline_margin": -8.21, "analyst_adjustment": 0.21, "key_factors": ["R+8 district lean with incumbent Brad Knott running for reelection", "Expert ratings cluster Safe/Likely R across ten outlets", "DCCC added the district to its target list and Barringer has raised over $1 million", "No public general-election polling in the race"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point clearly to Knott: an R+8 lean, incumbency advantage, and a consensus of Safe-to-Likely R ratings, with markets pricing Democratic chances near 15%. Barringer's strong fundraising and national Democratic targeting justify only a modest nudge toward competitiveness, not a reclassification.", "watch": "A late influx of outside spending against Knott or polling showing a single-digit race amid a weakening national environment for Republicans could move this toward Lean R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Brad Knott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 13th District leans Republican by about eight points, and first-term incumbent Brad Knott is seeking reelection with a healthy $700,000 war chest. Democratic nominee Paul Barringer has kept pace financially, raising over $1 million, and national Democrats have added the district to their target list, signaling they see an opening in a year when Trump's approval has softened even in red-leaning North Carolina districts.\n\nStill, the structural math is steep. Ten expert ratings range from Likely R to Safe R, and prediction markets give Barringer only about a one-in-seven chance. With no public general-election polls, the forecast rests on fundamentals and ratings, both of which favor Knott by a clear but not insurmountable margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0835, "margin": -8.64}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0743, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1064, "margin":